.TH WAVE-BASEDIR 1 "" "MacWave" "MacWave Manual"

.SH NAME
wave-basedir \- Print MacWave's install root, which is where the binaries, the links and the dependencies live. It is fixed: /opt/macwave on macOS and /home/linuxwave/.linuxwave on Linux, with no configuration file involved. A path under your home directory is shown with ~. With --raw it prints the absolute path as-is and nothing else.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wave basedir [\-\-raw]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Print MacWave's install root, which is where the binaries, the links and
the dependencies live. It is fixed: /opt/macwave on macOS and
/home/linuxwave/.linuxwave on Linux, with no configuration file
involved. A path under your home directory is shown with ~. With --raw
it prints the absolute path as-is and nothing else.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-raw
Print the absolute path as-is, with no emoji or colour (for scripts)

.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
wave basedir
wave basedir --raw
cd "$(wave basedir --raw)"
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
wave(1)
